```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a missing BGP network advertisement on router1. Router1's FRR
configuration defines the eBGP session to router2 (AS 2) but contains no
`network` statement under the BGP address-family, so it never originates a
prefix for its directly connected LAN 195.11.14.0/24 into BGP. Router2
therefore has zero BGP-learned routes from AS 1 — its entire routing table
for remote destinations consists only of its own locally originated
200.1.1.0/24. This explains the precise asymmetry of symptoms:

• pc2 → pc1 times out immediately with "Destination Net Unreachable" from
  router2 (200.1.1.1): router2 has no route to 195.11.14.0/24 at all, so it
  generates an ICMP unreachable the instant pc2's packet arrives.

• pc1 → pc2 times out silently: router1 *does* have a BGP-learned route to
  200.1.1.0/24 via router2, so pc1's ICMP echo requests are forwarded all the
  way to pc2. However, pc2's echo replies are forwarded to router2, which still
  has no return path to 195.11.14.0/24, so the replies are black-holed and
  pc1 sees only timeouts.

The eBGP session itself is fully Established (confirmed via `show bgp ipv4
unicast summary` on both routers), which is why the fault is subtle — the
control plane looks healthy but the advertisement is absent.

Root cause: router1's BGP process has no `network 195.11.14.0/24` statement,
so it never advertises its LAN prefix to AS 2.

Fix: Under router1's BGP configuration add:
  address-family ipv4 unicast
    network 195.11.14.0/24
  exit-address-family
This will originate the prefix into BGP, router2 will install a BGP route to
195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1, and bidirectional reachability will be
restored.
=== END ===
